message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "The minimum number of distinct abelian squares in a binary circular word"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Bhattacharjee
    given-names: Deep
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0466-750X"
    affiliation: "Formerly, Electro-Gravitational Space Propulsion Laboratory (EGSPL), Bhubaneswar, Odisha 751030, India"
    email: itsdeep@live.com
version: 2.3.0
date-released: 2026-10-27
url: "https://github.com/creelie/literate-octo-lamp/releases/tag/v2.3.0"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/creelie/literate-octo-lamp"
keywords:
  - abelian square
  - circular word
  - Parikh vector
  - combinatorics on words
  - Lean 4
abstract: >-
  We prove that every binary circular word of length n contains at least
  floor((n-1)/2) distinct abelian squares, and we determine all words
  attaining this bound. This settles Conjecture 0.6 of Simpson: its odd case
  holds, and in its even case the list of extremal words misses (ab)^{n/2}
  for n = 2 (mod 4), n >= 6. The proofs are formalized in Lean 4.
  Version 2.3.0 reviews the other problems of Simpson's survey: lemmas and
  a conjecture, verified to length 27, on the least number of non-equivalent
  abelian squares in an odd-length circular word, and a theorem that the
  least number of occurrences of abelian squares in a binary word of length
  n is asymptotically proportional to n or grows faster, and is below n^{3/2}.
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Solved and unsolved problems about abelian squares"
    authors:
      - family-names: Simpson
        given-names: Jamie
    year: 2018
